== 3. Advice and Counsel in Eleven Particulars

Now, for the better management of this great duty, closet prayer, I beg you#super[+] to take my advice and counsel in these eleven particulars that follow:

=== a. Be Frequent in Closet Prayer, and Not Now and Then Only

First, be frequent in closet prayer, and not now and then only. He who is not frequent in closet prayer will never make any earnings from closet prayer. Now, so that this counsel may stick, consider:

\[1\] First, other eminent servants of the Lord have been frequent in this blessed work. “Let Your eyes be open and Your ears attentive to hear the prayer that I, Your servant, now pray before You day and night” (Neh 1:6). So Daniel: “three times a day he got down on his knees, prayed, and gave thanks to his God, just as he had done before” (Dan 6:10). So David: “In the morning, O #smallcaps[Lord], You hear my voice; in the morning I will direct my prayer to You and will look up” (Ps 5:3). “But to You, O #smallcaps[Lord], I cry for help; in the morning my prayer comes before You” (Ps 88:13). “I rise before dawn and cry for help” (Ps 119:147). “Evening, morning, and noon I pray and cry aloud” (Ps 55:17). Indeed, he was #emph[vir orationis], “a man of prayer,” for his frequency in it. “In return for my love they accuse me, but I give myself to prayer” (Ps 109:4), or, as the Hebrew may be read, “But I am a man of prayer.”

Of Carolus Magnus it was said, #emph[Carolus plus cum Deo quam hominibus loquitur], that he spoke more with God than with men.

\[2\] Secondly, consider that the blessed Scripture not only commands this duty but requires frequency in it as well (Luke 18:1; 1 Thess 5:17; Col 4:2). In the former part of this discourse, I have shed light on these scriptures, and therefore the bare citing of them must now suffice.

\[3\] Thirdly, Christ was frequent in private prayer, as you may easily see by comparing these scriptures together (Mark 1:35; Matt 14:23; Luke 22:39; John 18:2). In my second argument for private prayer, you may see these scriptures opened and amplified.

\[4\] Fourthly, consider that you#super[+] have the examples of the very worst of men in this case. Papists are frequent in their private devotions. And the Muslims, whatever occasion they have to divert them, either by profit or pleasure, will still pray five times every day. Indeed, the very heathens sacrificed to Hercules morning and evening on the great altar at Rome. Now, shall blind nature do more than grace?

\[5\] Fifthly, consider that you#super[+] cannot have too frequent communion with God. You#super[+] cannot have too frequent fellowship with Jesus. You#super[+] cannot have your hearts too frequently filled with joy unspeakable and full of glory, and with that peace that passes understanding. You#super[+] cannot have heaven too frequently brought down into your hearts, nor can you have your hearts too frequently carried up to heaven. And therefore you#super[+] cannot be too frequent in closet prayer.

\[6\] Sixthly, consider that you#super[+] are under frequent needs, and frequent sins, and frequent snares, and frequent temptations, and frequent allurements, and frequent trials, and frequent cares, and frequent fears, and frequent favors (1 Pet 5:8; Job 1:7). And therefore you#super[+] need to be frequent with God in your closets.

\[7\] Seventhly, consider that you#super[+] are the favorites of heaven. You#super[+] are greatly beloved, you are highly honored, you are exceedingly esteemed and valued in the court of the Most High. And remember that the petitions of many weak Christians, and of many benighted Christians, and of many tempted Christians, and of many clouded Christians, and of many staggering Christians, and of many doubting Christians, and of many bewildered Christians, and of many fainting Christians, etc., are put into your#super[+] hands for a quick and speedy dispatch to the throne of grace. So you#super[+] need to be frequent in your closets and make use of your influence in heaven, or else many of these poor hearts may be wronged, betrayed, and harmed by your neglect. Those who are favorites in princes’ courts, if they are active, diligent, careful, and watchful, may do much good for others. They may come into their prince’s presence as often as they please, and, like Queen Esther, have whatever they please for the asking, both for themselves and others (Est 7). Oh, what a world of good may those who are God’s favorites do for others, if they would only be frequent with God in their closets!

O sirs! If you#super[+] do not have that love, that regard, that pity, that compassion for your own souls that you should have, yet, oh, do not let others suffer by your neglect of private prayer! Oh, do not let Zion suffer! Oh, do not let any particular saint suffer by your#super[+] being seldom found in your closets.

Certainly, it might have gone better with the churches of Christ, and with the concerns of Christ, and with many of the poor people of Christ, if most Christians had been more frequent with God in their closets.

\[8\] Eighthly and lastly, consider that this liberty to draw near to God in your#super[+] closets cost Christ His dearest blood (Eph 2:13; Heb 10:20). Now, he who is not frequent with God in his closet tells everyone around him that he sets no great value on the liberty that Christ has purchased with His blood. The incomparable, the unparalleled price that Christ paid down in full more than sixteen hundred years ago, so that we might have liberty and free access to His Father in our closets, shows very strongly, indeed irrefutably, the superlative excellence of that liberty (1 Pet 1:19). Oh, therefore let us make good use of this blessed purchase of our Lord Jesus by being frequent with God in our closets.

Some dispute whether one drop of Christ’s blood was sufficient for the pardon of our sins and the redemption of our souls. My intention is not to dispute but to offer a few things for your#super[+] consideration:

First, it must be granted that, by reason of the hypostatic union, a drop of Christ’s blood was of inestimable worth and excellence; and the value of His passion is to be measured by the dignity of His person.

Secondly, a proportion was to be observed between the punishment due to men and that which was suffered for man. For His sufferings to make satisfaction, two things were necessary: #emph[poenae gravitas], the gravity of the punishment, as well as #emph[personae dignitas], the dignity of the person.

That the least drop of Christ’s blood was not sufficient for the redemption of our souls may be shown in this way:

First, if it were, then the circumcision of Christ was enough, for there a drop of blood was shed, if not many drops.

Secondly, then His being crowned with a crown of thorns was sufficient, for it is most probable that they drew blood from Him.

Thirdly, then all Christ’s other sufferings were superfluous and vain.

Fourthly, then God was unjust and unrighteous to take more than was due to His justice. But for any man to affirm that God has taken beyond what was His just due is high blasphemy.

Fifthly, then Christ was weak and imprudent to pay more than He needed, for what need was there of His dearest heart’s blood if a drop from His hand would have saved our souls? Let the schoolmen imagine what they please, it is certain that not one dram of that bitter cup that Christ drank down could be abated, if His Father was to be fully satisfied and man eternally redeemed. Christ has declared under His own hand that it was necessary for Him to suffer many things (Luke 24:26; 17:25). O sirs! Shall Christ shed not only a few drops of blood, but His very heart’s blood, to purchase for you#super[+] a freedom and liberty to be in your closets with His Father as often as you please, and will you give God a visit in private only now and then? The Lord forbid.

=== b. Take the Fittest Seasons and Opportunities for Closet Prayer

My second advice and counsel is this: take #emph[the fittest seasons and opportunities] that you#super[+] possibly can for closet prayer. Many take unfit seasons for private prayer, which obstruct the importunity of the soul in prayer more than all the suggestions and instigations of Satan. For example:

First, when the body is drowsy and sleepy, this is a very unfit season for closet prayer (Song 3:1). Take heed of laying cushions of sloth under your#super[+] knees, or pillows of idleness under your elbows, or of mixing nods with your petitions, or of being drowsily devout when you draw near to God in your closets.

Secondly, when a man’s head and heart are filled with worldly cares and distractions, this is a very unfit season for closet prayer (1 Cor 7:35; Ezek 33:31).

When Dinah must go gadding about to see the fashions, Shechem, the prince of that country, meets with her and violates her. So when our hearts, like Dinah, must go roving and gadding about after the things of the world, then Satan, the prince of the air, usually seizes on us and commits a rape upon our souls. He either leads us away from prayer, or else he so distracts us in prayer that it would be better not to have prayed at all than to have offered the sacrifice of foolish and distracted prayer.

I have read a story of how one man offered to give his horse to his companion on condition that he would only say the Lord’s Prayer and think of nothing but God. The offer was accepted, and he began, “‘Our Father in heaven, hallowed be Your name’ (Matt 6:9)…But I must have the bridle too,” he said. “No, nor the horse either,” said the other, “for you have lost both already.” The application is easy.

Certainly the freest and liveliest season for closet prayer is the morning, before a man’s spirit is blunted or cooled, deadened, damped, or dulled by worldly business. A man should speak with God in his closet before he speaks with his worldly affairs and dealings. A man should say to all his worldly business what Abraham said to his young men when he went to offer up his only Isaac: “Stay here…I…will go over there to worship, and then…return to you#super[+]” (Gen 22:5). He who would attend to closet prayer without distraction or disturbance must not first slip out of the world into his closet, but he must first slip into his closet before he is surrounded by a crowd of worldly employments.

It was a precept of Pythagoras that when we enter the temple to worship God, we must not so much as speak or think of any worldly business, lest we make God’s service an idle, perfunctory, and lazy recreation. I may say the same of closet prayer.

Jerome complains very much of his distractions, dullness, and unfitness for prayer, and chides himself in this way: “What! Do you think that Jonah prayed like this when he was in the whale’s belly, or Daniel when he was among the lions, or the thief when he was on the cross?”

Thirdly, when men or women are in rash and angry disorders (1 Tim 2:8), this is a very unfit season for closet prayer, for when passions are up, holy affections are down. For prayers that do not first warm our own hearts will never reach God’s ear. In the Muscovite churches, if the minister makes a mistake in reading, or stammers in pronouncing his words, or speaks any word that is not heard well, the hearers blame him very much and are ready to take the book from him, as unworthy to read from it. And certainly God is no less offended with the giddy, rash, heated, precipitate, and inconsiderate prayers of those who, without deliberate understanding, send their petitions to heaven in great haste. Solomon’s advice is worthy of all praise and acceptance: “Do not be rash with your mouth, and do not be hasty in your heart to utter a word before God” (Eccl 5:2). Or, as the Hebrew may be read, “Do not let your heart, through haste, be so troubled or disturbed as to tumble over and throw out words without wisdom or forethought.” Good men are often apt to be too hasty, rash, and unadvised in their prayers, complaints, and pleas against evil. Witness David, Job, Jeremiah, Jonah, and the disciples.

There is no Christian like the one who wisely and seriously weighs his prayers and praises before he pours out his soul before the Lord. He who first duly deliberates what to request never repents of his requests. But he who blurts out whatever lies uppermost, and who brings into the presence of God his rash, raw, tumultuous, and undigested petitions, confessions, complaints, etc., only provokes God. He only brawls with God, instead of praying to Him or wrestling with Him. Suitors at court watch for their fittest times and seasons for begging. They commonly take the very nick of time when they have the king in a good mood, and so seldom or never come away without good success.

Sometimes God strongly inclines the heart to closet prayer. Sometimes He brings the heart beforehand into a praying frame; sometimes both body and soul are more enlivened, revived, raised, and divinely inflamed than at other times. Sometimes conscience is more stirring, working, and tender. Oh, now strike while the iron is hot! Oh, now lay hold on all such blessed opportunities by applying yourself to private prayer. O sirs! Can you#super[+] take your fittest times, seasons, and opportunities for plowing, and sowing, and reaping, and buying and selling, and eating, and drinking, and marrying, and can you not just as well take your fittest times and seasons to seek the Lord in your closets? Must the best God be put off with the least and worst of your#super[+] time? The Lord forbid. Do not neglect the seasons of grace; do not let your#super[+] opportunities for closet prayer slip. Thousands have lost their seasons and their souls together.

=== c. Do Not Perform Closet Duties Merely to Still Your Consciences

My third advice and counsel is this: be exceedingly careful that you#super[+] do not perform closet duties merely to still your consciences. You#super[+] must perform them out of conscience, but you must not perform them only to quiet conscience. Some have such a light set up in their understandings that they cannot omit closet prayer without conscience being on their backs, always upbraiding and disquieting them. And therefore they are afraid to neglect closet prayer, lest conscience should question, arraign, and condemn them for their neglect. Sometimes, when men have greatly sinned against the Lord, conscience becomes impatient and is always accusing, condemning, and terrifying them. And now, in these agonies, they will run to their closets, and cry, and pray, and mourn, and confess, and bitterly bewail their transgressions. But all this is only to quiet their consciences. And sometimes they find, on performing closet duties, that their consciences are a little calmed and quieted; and for this very end and purpose they take up closet prayer as a charm to calm their consciences. And when the storm is over and their consciences are quieted, they lay closet prayer aside—as the monk did the net when the fish was caught—and are ready to transgress again. O sirs! Take heed of this, for this is nothing but plain hypocrisy, and it will be bitterness in the end. He who performs closet prayer only to bribe his conscience so that it will not be clamorous, or to stop the mouth of conscience so that it will not accuse him for sin, will at length venture on such a trade, such a course of sinning against conscience, as will certainly turn his troubled conscience into a seared conscience (1 Tim 4:2). And a seared conscience is like a sleeping lion: when he awakes, he roars and tears his prey in pieces. So a seared conscience, when it is awakened, will roar and tear the complacent sinner in pieces.

When Dionysius’s conscience was awakened, he was so troubled with fear and horror of conscience that, not daring to trust his best friends with a razor, he used to singe his beard with burning coals, as Cicero reports. All the mercy that a seared, a benumbed, conscience affords the sinner, when it most befriends him, when it deals most seemingly kindly with him, is this: it will not cut, so that it may kill; it will not convict, so that it may confound; it will not accuse, so that it may condemn. It will spare the sinner a while so that it may torment him forever; it will spare him here so that it may gnaw him hereafter. It will not strike until it is too late for the sinner to ward off the blow. Oh, cruel mercy, to see the sin and leave the sinner alone until the gates of mercy are shut on him and hell stands gaping to devour him! “Sin #emph[lies] at the door” (Gen 4:7). The Hebrew word #emph[robets] signifies to lie down, or couch, like some wild beast at the mouth of its cave, as if it were asleep, but in fact it watches and wakes, and is ready to fly at all who come near it. O sirs! Sin is rather #emph[couchant] (lying down) than #emph[dormant] (sleeping). It sleeps a dog’s sleep so that it may take the sinner at the greater advantage and fly the more furiously in his face.

=== d. Take Heed of Resting and Trusting upon Closet Duties

My fourth advice and counsel is this: take heed of resting on closet duties; take heed of trusting in closet duties. Noah’s dove made use of her wings, but she did not trust in her wings, but in the ark. So you#super[+] must make use of closet duties, but you must not trust in your closet duties, but in Jesus, of Whom the ark was only a type.

There are many who go a round of duties as mill horses go their round in a mill, and rest on them when they have done, using the means as mediators, and so fall short of Christ and heaven at once. Closet duties rested in will undo a man eternally, as surely as the greatest and foulest enormities. Open wickedness slays her thousands, but a secret resting on duties slays her ten thousands. Multitudes bleed inwardly of this disease and die forever. Open profaneness is the broad, dirty way that leads to hell, but closet duties rested in are a sure way to hell, though a cleaner way. Profane persons and professing Christians of mere outward form shall meet at last in one hell. Ah, Christians! Do not make closet duties your#super[+] money, lest you and your money perish together.

The phoenix gathers sweet, fragrant sticks together in Arabia, and then fans them with her wings and burns herself with them. So many shining professing Christians burn themselves by resting in their duties and services. You#super[+] know that in Noah’s Flood all who were not in the ark, though they climbed up the tallest trees and the highest mountains and hills, were really drowned all the same. So let men climb up to this duty and that; yet if they do not get into Christ, they will really be damned. It is not your closet, but your Christ, that must save you. If a man has no share in Christ, he may perish with “Our Father” in his mouth. It is as natural for a man to rest in his duties as it is for him to rest in his bed.

This was Bernard’s temptation: when he had been helped a little in duty, he could stroke his own head with #emph[bene fecisti Bernard], “O Bernard, this was gallantly done; now cheer yourself up.” Ah, how apt man is, when he has been a little helped, heated, melted, set free in a way of duty, to go away and stroke himself, bless himself, hug himself, and warm himself with the sparks, with the fire of his own kindling (Isa 50:11)!

Adam was to win life and wear it; he was to be saved by his doings. Do this and live (Gen 2:2). That is why all his descendants are so prone to seek salvation by doing (Acts 2:37). “What must I do to be saved?” (Acts 16:30). “Good Teacher, what must I do to inherit eternal life?” (Mark 10:17–20). Like father, like son. But if our own duties or doings were sufficient to save us, to what purpose did Christ leave His Father’s bosom and lay down His dearest life?

Closet duties rested in may pacify conscience for a time, but this will not always hold. “When Ephraim saw his sickness and Judah his wound, then Ephraim turned to Assyria and sent to King Jareb. But he cannot cure you#super[+] or heal your wound” (Hos 5:13).

If we rest on closet duties, or on anything else this side of Christ, we shall find them as weak as the Assyrian, or as Jareb. We shall find to our cost that they cannot help us or heal us; they cannot comfort us or cure us of our wounds. As created things were never true to any who trusted in them, so neither were duties. When the Israelites were in great distress, the Lord tells them, “Go and cry out to the gods you#super[+] have chosen. Let them save you#super[+],” says God, “in your time of trouble” (Judg 10:14). O sirs! If, when you#super[+] are in distress of conscience, or lying on a deathbed, God should say to you, Go to your closet prayers and performances that you have made and rested in; go to your closet tears that you have shed and rested in, and let them save you and deliver you—oh, what miserable saviors and comforters they would be to you! What the ark of God was to the Philistines (1 Sam 5), closet duties are to Satan. He trembles every time he sees a poor sinner go into his closet and come out of his closet resting and glorying in Jesus, and not in his duties. But when he sees a poor creature confide in his closet duties and rest on his closet duties, then he rejoices; then he claps his hands and sings, “Aha! That is how I would have it.”

Oh, do not rest on anything this side of Jesus Christ. Say to your#super[+] graces, say to your duties, say to your holiness, You are not my savior, you are not my mediator; and therefore you are not to be relied on, you are not to be rested in. It is my duty to perform closet duties, but it is my sin to rely on them, or to put confidence in them. Do them I must, but glory in them I must not. He who rests in his closet duties makes a savior of his closet duties. Let all your#super[+] closet duties lead you to Jesus and leave you more in communion with Him and in dependence on Him, and then you will be thrice happy (Heb 7:25). Let all your closet prayers and tears, your closet fastings and meltings, be a star to guide you to Jesus, a Jacob’s ladder by which you may ascend into the bosom of eternal loves, and then you are safe forever.

Ah! It is sad to think how most men have forgotten their resting place, as the Lord complains: “My people are lost sheep; their shepherds have led them astray, causing them to roam the mountains. They have wandered from mountain to hill; they have forgotten their resting place” (Jer 50:6). Ah, how many poor souls there are that wander from mountain to hill, from one duty to another, and here they will rest, and there they will rest, and all this side of their resting place! O sirs! It is God Himself who is your#super[+] resting place. It is His free grace, it is His singular mercy, it is His infinite love that is your#super[+] resting place. It is the bosom of Christ, the favor of Christ, the satisfaction of Christ, and the pure, perfect, spotless, matchless, and glorious righteousness of Christ that is your#super[+] resting place. And therefore say to all your#super[+] closet duties and performances, Farewell! Prayer, farewell! Reading, farewell! Fasting, farewell! Tears, farewell! Sighs and groans, farewell! Meltings and humblings, I will never trust you#super[+] again. I will never rest on you#super[+] again, but I will now return to my resting place. I will now rest only in God and Christ. I will now rest wholly in God and Christ. I will now rest forever in God and Christ.

It was the saying of a precious saint that he was more afraid of his duties than of his sins, for the one often made him proud, the other always made him humble.

=== e. Labor to Bring Your Hearts into All Your Closet Prayers

My fifth advice and counsel is this: labor to bring your#super[+] hearts into all your closet prayers and performances. See that your#super[+] tongues and your hearts keep time and tune. “Give ear to my prayer—it does not come from feigned lips” (Ps 17:1), or, as it is in the Hebrew, “without lips of deceit.” Heart and tongue must go together; word and work, lip and life, prayer and practice, must echo one another, or else your prayers and your soul will be lost together. The labor of the lips and the travail of the heart must go together.

The Egyptians, of all fruits, chose the peach to consecrate to their goddess, for no other reason than that its fruit is like a heart and its leaf like a tongue. These very heathens, in the worship of their gods, thought it necessary that men’s hearts and tongues should go together. Ah, Christians! When your#super[+] hearts and your tongues go together in your closet duties, then you make that sweet and delightful melody that is most winning and pleasing to the King of kings. The very soul of prayer lies in the pouring out of the soul before God (1 Sam 1:15). “These things come to mind as I pour out my soul” (Ps 42:4). So the Israelites poured out their souls like water before the Lord. So the church: “Your name and renown are the desire of our souls. My soul longs for You in the night; indeed, my spirit seeks You at dawn” (Isa 26:8–9). “Let us lift up our hearts and hands to God in heaven” (Lam 3:41). “Let us draw near with a sincere heart” (Heb 10:22). “God, whom I serve with my spirit … is my witness” (Rom 1:9). “I will pray with my spirit, … I will sing with my spirit” (1 Cor 14:15). “It is we who are the circumcision, we who worship God in the spirit” (Phil 3:3).

Under the law only the inward parts were to be offered to God in sacrifice; the skin belonged to the priests. From this we may easily gather that truth in the inward parts is what is most pleasing in a sacrifice. When the Athenians asked the oracle the cause of their frequent lack of success in battle against the Lacedaemonians, seeing that they offered in sacrifice to the gods the choicest things they could get, which their enemies did not, the oracle gave them this answer: the gods were better pleased with their enemies’ inward supplication without ambition than with all the Athenians’ outward pomp in costly sacrifices.

Ah, sirs! The reason why so many are so unsuccessful in their closet duties and services is that there is no more of their hearts in them. No man can make sure work or happy work of prayer except the one who makes heart work of it. When a man’s heart is in his prayers, then great and sweet will be his returns from heaven. That is no prayer in which the heart of the person bears no part. When the soul is separated from the body, the man is dead; and so, when the heart is separated from the lip in prayer, the prayer is dead.

The Jews to this day write these words on the walls of their synagogues, #emph[Tephillah belo cavannah ceguph belo neshamah]\; that is, a prayer without the heart, or without the intention of the affection, is like a body without a soul.

In the Law of Moses, the priest was commanded to wash the inward parts and the feet of the sacrifices in water; and this was done, says Philo, “not without a mystery, to teach us to keep our hearts and affections clean when we draw near to God.” In all your#super[+] closet duties God looks first and most to your hearts: “My son, give me your heart” (Prov 23:26). It is not a piece, it is not a corner of the heart, that will satisfy the Maker of the heart. The heart is a treasure, a bed of spices, a royal throne in which He delights. God does not look at the elegance of your#super[+] prayers to see how neat they are, nor yet at the geometry of your prayers to see how long they are, nor yet at the arithmetic of your prayers to see how many they are, nor yet at the music of your prayers, nor yet at the sweetness of your voice, nor yet at the logic of your prayers, but at the sincerity of your prayers, how heartfelt they are. There is no prayer acknowledged, approved, accepted, recorded, or rewarded by God except the one in which the heart is, sincerely and wholly. The true mother would not have the child divided. As God loves “a broken and a contrite heart,” so He loathes a divided heart (Ps 51:17; Jas 1:8).

God loves neither halting nor halving. He will be served truly and totally. The royal law is, “You shall love and serve the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul.” Among the heathens, when the beasts were cut up for sacrifice, the first thing the priest looked at was the heart, and if the heart was bad, the sacrifice was rejected. Truly, God rejects all those services and sacrifices in which the heart is not, as you may see by comparing these scriptures together (Prov 21:27; Isa 1:11–12; 29:13; Matt 15:7–9; Ezek 33:30–32; Zech 7:4–6; 2 Chr 25:1–2; Ps 78:36–37). Prayer without the heart is only like sounding brass or a clanging cymbal. Prayer is lovely and weighty only as the heart is in it, and not otherwise. It is not the lifting up of the voice, the wringing of the hands, the beating of the breast, an affected tone, studied notions, or seraphic expressions, but the stirrings of the heart that God looks at in prayer. God hears no more than the heart speaks. If the heart is dumb, God will certainly be deaf. No prayer finds favor with God except the one that is the travail of the heart.

On the same day that Julius Caesar came to the imperial dignity, sitting in his golden chair, he offered a beast in sacrifice to the gods. But when the beast was opened, it was without a heart, which the soothsayers looked on as an ill omen. It is a sad omen that you will rather provoke the Lord than prevail with Him, if you are habitually heartless in your closet duties. Of the heart, God seems to say to us, as Joseph did to his brothers concerning Benjamin, “You#super[+] will not see my face without it.”

It was the saying of blessed Bradford that he would never leave a duty until he had brought his heart into the frame of the duty. He would not leave confession of sin until his heart was broken for sin. He would not leave petitioning for grace until his heart was revived and enlivened in a hopeful expectation of more grace. He would not leave thanksgiving until his heart was enlarged with a sense of the blessings he enjoyed and revived to return praise.

=== f. Be Fervent, Be Warm, Be Importunate with God

My sixth advice and counsel is this: Be fervent, be warm, be importunate with God in all your#super[+] closet duties and exercises. “The effectual, fervent prayer of a righteous man has great power” (Jas 5:16)—or, as the Greek has it, “the working prayer.” That is, such working prayer as sets the whole man to work, as sets all the faculties of the soul and all the graces in the soul at work. The word signifies such a working as denotes the liveliest activity there can be. Certainly, all those common phrases of crying, wrestling, and striving with God that are scattered up and down in Scripture strongly show the holy importunity and sacred violence that the saints of old expressed in their addresses to God.

Fervency feathers the wings of prayer and makes them fly the swifter to heaven. An arrow drawn back only a little way does not fly far; but if it is drawn back to the head, it will fly far and pierce deeply. So fervent prayer flies as high as heaven and will certainly bring down blessings from there. Cold prayers call for a denial, but fervent prayers offer a sacred violence both to heaven and earth. Just as in a painted fire there is no heat, so in a cold prayer there is no heat, no warmth, no omnipotence, no devotion, no blessing. Cold prayers are like arrows without heads, like swords without edges, like birds without wings. They do not pierce, they do not cut, they do not fly up to heaven. Prayers that have no heavenly fire in them always freeze before they reach as high as heaven. But fervent prayer has great prevailing power with God.

“So Peter was kept in prison, but the church was fervently praying” (Acts 12:5). The Greek word ἐκτενής signifies urgent prayer, earnest prayer, stretched-out prayer—prayer stretched out on the tenter frame, as it were. These gracious souls strained and stretched themselves in prayer as men do who are running in a race. They prayed with all the strength of their souls and with all the fervency of their spirits; and accordingly they carried the day with God, as you may see in the verses that follow.

“… our twelve tribes … earnestly serve God day and night” (Acts 26:7), or rather, as the Greek has it, ἐν ἐκτενείᾳ, “in a stretched-out manner, serving God day and night.” These twelve tribes, or the godly Jews of the twelve tribes of Israel, stretched out their hearts, their affections, their graces, to the utmost in prayer. In all your#super[+] private retreats, do as the twelve tribes did.

“Fervent in spirit, serving the Lord” (Rom 12:11). The Greek word ζέοντες signifies “seething hot.” God loves to see His people zealous and warm in His service. Without fervency of spirit no service finds acceptance in heaven. God is a pure act, and He loves His people to be lively and active in His service.

“Persistent in prayer” (Rom 12:12); προσκαρτεροῦντες, continuing with all your#super[+] might in prayer. It is a metaphor from hunting dogs that will never give up the chase until they have caught their game.

“to strive together with me in your#super[+] prayers to God for me” (Rom 15:30); συναγωνίσασθαι, strive mightily, strive as champions strive, even to an agony, as the word implies. It is a military word, and denotes such fervent wrestling or striving as is for life and death.

“Always wrestling in prayer for you#super[+]” (Col 4:12). The Greek word used here, ἀγωνιζόμενος, signifies to strive or wrestle as those do who strive for mastery. It denotes the vehemence and fervor of Epaphras’ prayers for the Colossians. Just as wrestlers bend, and writhe, and stretch, and strain every joint of their bodies so that they may be victorious, so Epaphras bent, and writhed, and stretched, and strained every joint of his soul—if I may speak so—so that he might be victorious with God on the Colossians’ behalf.

So, when Jacob was alone with God, ah, how earnest and fervent he was in his wrestlings with God (Gen 32:24–27; Hos 12:4–5)! He wrestles and weeps, and weeps and wrestles. He tugs hard with God, he keeps his hold, and he will not let God go, until, as a prince, he had prevailed with Him.

Fervent prayer is the soul’s contention, the soul struggling with God. It is sweating work; it is the sweat and blood of the soul; it is a laying out to the uttermost of all the strength and powers of the soul. He who would gain the victory over God in private prayer must strain every string of his heart. In beseeching God, he must besiege Him, and so get the better of Him. He must be like importunate beggars who will not be put off with frowns, or silence, or sad answers. Those who would be masters of their requests must, like the importunate widow, press God so far as to put Him to a holy blush, as I may say with reverence. With a holy impudence, as Basil puts it, they must make God ashamed to look them in the face if He should deny the importunity of their souls.

Had Abraham had a little more of this impudence, says one, when he pleaded for Sodom, it might have gone well. Abraham brought the price down to ten righteous, and there his modesty stopped him. Had he gone lower, God only knows what might have been done; for God did not go away, says the text, until “He had finished speaking with Abraham” (Gen 18:33). That is, until Abraham had no more to say to God. Abraham left off asking before God left off granting. He left off praying before God left off lowering the price; and so Sodom was lost.

Oh, the heavenly fire, the holy fervency that was in Daniel’s closet prayer! “O Lord, listen! O Lord, forgive! O Lord, hear and act! For Your sake … do not delay” (Dan 9:19). Just as there are two kinds of antidotes against poison, hot and cold, so there are two kinds of antidotes against all the troubles of this life, fervent prayers and holy patience—the one hot, the other cold; the one enlivening, and the other quenching. And holy Daniel made use of them both. Fervency is to prayer as the fire was to the spices in the censer, or as wings to the bird, or as oil to the wheels; and Daniel found this by experience.

God does not look for any James with calloused knees from constant prayer, nor for any Bartholomew with a hundred prayers for the morning and as many for the evening, but for fervency of spirit in prayer, which alone carries all with God. Feeble prayers, like weak labor pains, pass off and never bring a mercy to birth. Cold prayers are stillborn children in whom the Father of spirits can take no pleasure. Just as a painted man is no man, and painted fire is no fire, so a cold prayer is no prayer. Prayers that do not first warm our own hearts never win the heart of God. As a body without a soul, much wood without a fire, a bullet in a gun without powder, so are all prayers without fervency of spirit.

Luther calls prayer #emph[bombarda Christianorum], the gun or cannon of Christians, or the Christian’s gunshot.

The hottest springs send out their waters by boiling up. Cold prayers make a smoke, a choking smoke, in the eyes of God. Lazy prayers never obtain noble answers. Lazy beggars may starve for all their begging (Isa 1:15; 65:5).

Those who have a male in their flock and offer the Lord a female; those who offer the Lord the torn and the lame and the sick; those who put God off with their cold, lazy, sleepy, and formal devotions are condemned, convicted, and cursed by God (Mal 1:13–14). David compares his prayers to incense, and no incense was offered without fire (Ps 141:2). It was the fire that made its smoke ascend. It is only fervent prayer that hits the mark and pierces the walls of heaven, though they are made of brass and iron like those of Gaza (Isa 45:2). While the child only whimpers and whines in the cradle, the mother lets it alone; but once it raises its voice and cries outright, then she runs and picks it up. So it is with a Christian: “This poor man cried out” (Ps 34:6). There is his fervency: he cried out. But it was silently and secretly, in the presence of King Achish, as Moses did at the Red Sea, and as Nehemiah did in the presence of the king of Persia. “And the #smallcaps[Lord] heard him; He saved him from all his troubles” (Ps 34:6). Here is his prevailing power. So Latimer plied the throne of grace with great fervency, crying out, “Once again, Lord, once again restore the gospel to England,” and God heard him.

Hudson the martyr, deserted by God at the stake, slipped out from under his chain; and having prayed fervently, he was comforted at once and suffered valiantly.

I have read of one Giles of Brussels, a Dutch martyr, who was so fervent in his prayer, kneeling by himself in some secret place of the prison where he was, that he seemed to forget himself. When he was called to his food, he neither heard nor saw who stood by him until he was lifted up by the arms; and then he would speak gently to them, as one awakened out of a trance.

So Gregory Nazianzen, speaking of his sister Gorgonia, says that in the vehemence of her prayer she came to a religious impudence with God, so as to threaten heaven and tell God that she would never depart from His altar until her petition was granted.

Let us make it our business to follow these noble examples, if ever we would so play the prince in prayer as to prevail with God. An importunate soul in prayer is like the poor beggar who prays and knocks, who prays and waits, who prays and works, who knocks and knits, who begs and patches, and will not stir from the door until he has received alms. Well, friends, remember this: God regards lukewarm prayers no more than He does lukewarm persons, and they are such as He has threatened to spit out of His mouth. Prayers that are only lip-labor are lost labor; and therefore, in all your#super[+] closet prayers, look to the fervency of your spirits.

=== g. Be Constant, as Well as Fervent, in Closet Prayer

My seventh advice and counsel is this: Be constant, as well as fervent, in closet prayer. See that you#super[+] hold on and hold out, and that you persevere to the end in private prayer. “Pray without ceasing” (1 Thess 5:17). A man must always pray in habit, though not in act. He must have his heart in a praying disposition in all states and conditions. Though closet prayer may have an intermission, yet it must never have a cessation. “Then Jesus told them a parable about their need to pray at all times and not lose heart” (Luke 18:1), or, as the Greek has it, “not to shrink back, as sluggards in work or cowards in war.” Closet prayer is a fire like the one on the altar, which was never to go out day or night. “Night and day we pray most earnestly” (1 Thess 3:10). Paul speaks like a man made up entirely of prayer, like a man who minded nothing so much as prayer. “Pray in the Spirit always, with every kind of prayer and supplication. To this end, stay alert with all perseverance” (Eph 6:18). Calvin makes this difference between “praying always” at the beginning of this verse and “praying with perseverance” at the end of it: “By ‘praying always,’” he says, “he exhorts us to pray in prosperity as well as in adversity, and not to quit the duty of prayer in a prosperous state because we are not driven to it by outward pressing necessities and miseries. And by ‘praying with perseverance,’ he admonishes us not to be weary of the work, but to continue urgent and constant in its performance, though we do not at once receive what we pray for.” So “praying always” is opposed to a neglect of the duty in its proper times and seasons, and “praying with perseverance” is opposed to a losing of heart in our spirits over this or that particular request that we offer up to God. When God turns a deaf ear to our prayers, we must not fret or faint. We must not be dismayed or discouraged, but we must hold up and hold on in the duty of prayer with invincible patience, courage, and constancy, as the church did (Lam 3:8, 44, 55–57). “Devote yourselves to prayer, being watchful and thankful” (Col 4:2). We must be constant and urgent in closet prayer; we must wait on it and lay everything aside for it. He who is in his closet only by fits and starts will neither glorify God nor benefit his own soul. If we do not make a trade of closet prayer, we will never make any earnings from closet prayer. Just as those who make money by their ironworks keep a continual fire in their ironworks, so those who would get any good for their souls by closet duties must keep close and constant to closet duties. The hypocrite is constant only in inconstancy; he is in his closet only by fits and starts. Now and then, when he is in a good mood, you will find him stepping into his closet, but he never keeps at it. “Will he always call upon God?” (Job 27:10), or, as the Hebrew has it, “Will he call upon God at every time?” When they are under the smarting rod, or upon the tormenting rack, or under grievous needs, or struck with panic fears, etc., then you will see them run to their closets, as Joab ran to the horns of the altar when he was in danger of death. But they never persevere; they never hold out to the end. And therefore, in the end they lose both their closet prayers and their souls together (Isa 26:16; Ps 78:34; Zech 7:5).

It was a most profane and blasphemous speech of that atheistic wretch who told God “that he was no common beggar, and that he had never troubled Him with prayer before, and if He would only hear him this time, he would never trouble Him again.”

Closet prayer is hard work; and a man must tug hard at it and stick close to it, as Jacob did, if ever he intends to gain any internal or eternal advantage by it (Gen 32). Daniel chose to risk his life rather than give up praying in his room (Dan 6). It is not he who begins in the Spirit and ends in the flesh (Gal 3:3); it is not he who puts his hand to the plow and looks back (Luke 9:62); but he who perseveres to the end in prayer who will be saved and crowned (Matt 24:13). It is he who perseveres in doing good who will eat of the hidden manna, and who will have the white stone, “inscribed with a new name, known only to the one who receives it” (Rev 2:17). Those precious, praying, mourning souls in Ezekiel 9:4, 6 who were marked to be preserved in Jerusalem were distinguished, some of the learned say, by the letter ת, #emph[tau], the last of all the Hebrew letters, to teach them that they must hold out and hold on to the end in doing good. It is constancy in closet duty that crowns the Christian and commends the duty.

But would God have His people cast off their callings, and cast off all care of their families, and shut themselves up in their closets, and there spend their whole time in secret prayer? Oh, no! Every duty must have its time and place. And as one friend must not shut out another, so one duty must not shut out another (Eccl 3:1). The duties of my particular calling as I am a man must not shut out the duties of my general calling as I am a Christian. And the duties of my general calling as I am a Christian must not shut out the duties of my particular calling as I am a man. But so that you#super[+] may be fully satisfied in this case, you must remember that a man may be said to pray always,

\[1\] First, when his heart is always in a praying frame. A man may truly be said to give always whose heart is always in a giving frame, and to suffer always whose heart is always in a suffering frame—“For Your sake we face death all day long” (Ps 44:22)—and to sin always whose heart is always in a sinning frame (2 Pet 2:14; Jer 9:3). Just so, a man may as truly be said to pray always whose heart is always in a praying frame.

\[2\] Secondly, a man prays always when he takes hold of every fitting season and opportunity for pouring out his soul before the Lord in his closet. To pray always is ἐν παντὶ καιρῷ, to pray at every opportunity; but of this I have spoken before.

Some have observed of Proteus that he used to give certain oracles, but it was hard to make him speak and deliver them, for he would turn himself into various shapes and forms. Yet if they would hold out and press him hard without fear, whatever form or shape he appeared in, they were sure to have satisfying oracles. So if we continue constant in our closet wrestlings with God, if we hold on in private prayer, though God should appear to us in the form or shape of a judge, an enemy, a stranger, we will certainly succeed at last. “O woman, your faith is great! Let it be done for you as you desire.” And her daughter was healed from that very hour (Matt 15:28).

The philosopher, being asked in his old age why he did not give up his practice and take his ease, answered, “When a man is to run a race of forty furlongs, would you have him sit down at the thirty-ninth, and so lose the prize, the crown for which he ran?” O sirs! If you#super[+] do not hold out to the end in closet prayer, you will certainly lose the heavenly prize, the crown of life, the crown of righteousness, the crown of glory.

To continue in giving glory to God in this way of duty is as necessary and requisite as to begin to give glory to God in this way of duty; for though the beginning is more than half, yet the end is more than all. The finish crowns the work. The God of all perfections expects that our #emph[ultimum vitae], our life’s end, should be His #emph[optimum gloria], His best glory; that our last works should be our best works; and that we should persevere in closet prayer to the end (Rev 2:10).

=== h. Thirst and Long After Communion with God

My eighth advice and counsel is this: In all your#super[+] closet prayers, thirst and long after communion with God. In all your#super[+] private retreats, settle for nothing below fellowship with God, nothing below a sweet and spiritual enjoyment of God (Song 3:1–3; Ps 73:28). “One thing I have asked of the #smallcaps[Lord]; this is what I desire: to dwell in the house of the #smallcaps[Lord] all the days of my life, to gaze on the beauty of the #smallcaps[Lord] and seek Him in His temple” (Ps 27:4). The temple of the Lord without communion with the Lord of the temple will not satisfy David’s soul.

“As the deer pants for streams of water, so my soul longs after You, O God. My soul thirsts for God, the living God. When shall I come and appear in God’s presence?” (Ps 42:1–2). The deer, as Aristotle and others observe, is of all creatures the most hot and dry by nature; but especially when it is chased and hunted, then it is extremely thirsty. The female is meant here, as the Greek article, ἡ ἔλαφος, #emph[he elaphos], shows. Now, in the females the pangs of thirst are stronger, as the naturalists observe. By this David reveals what a vehement and inflamed thirst there was in his soul after communion with God; and as nothing could satisfy the hunted deer but the streams of water, so nothing could satisfy his soul but the enjoyment of God.

“Then I will go to the altar of God, to God, my greatest joy” (Ps 43:4). The altar of God is put here for the worship of God. Now, it is not merely the worship of God, but communion with God in His worship, that was David’s greatest joy. “O God, You are my God. Earnestly I seek You; my soul thirsts for You. My body yearns for You in a dry and weary land without water, to see Your power and Your glory, as I have seen You in the sanctuary” (Ps 63:1–2). David’s soul did not thirst after a crown, a kingdom, or any worldly greatness or glory, but after a choice and sweet enjoyment of God in his wilderness condition. Never did any woman with child long more after this or that than David’s soul longed to enjoy felt communion with God in the midst of all his sorrows and sufferings. “My soul longs, even faints, for the courts of the #smallcaps[Lord]; my heart and my flesh cry out for the living God” (Ps 84:2). By “the courts of the #smallcaps[Lord]” we are to understand the ordinances. Now, these without communion with God would never have satisfied David’s soul.

I commend that saying of Bernard, #emph[Nunquam abs te, absque te recedo]: I never come to You but by You; I never go from You without You.

Whenever you#super[+] go into your closets, press hard after real and felt communion with God, so that you may come out of your closets with some shining of God upon your spirits, as Moses came down from the mountain with his face shining (Exod 34:29–35). Oh, do not settle for your#super[+] closet prayers, or tears, or joys, or freedom of heart; but labor and long to enjoy such inward and close fellowship with God in your closets as may leave so choice and sweet a savor of God upon both your hearts and lives that others may be forced to say, Surely these have been with Jesus (Acts 4:13). It is sad when Christians return from their closets to their shops, their trades, their families, their business without the least visible rays of divine glory upon them.

O sirs! Closet prayer will be found to be only a dry, sapless, lifeless, heartless, comfortless thing if you#super[+] do not enjoy communion with God in it. Communion with God is the very life, soul, and crown of all your#super[+] closet duties; and therefore, press after it as for life. When you#super[+] go into your closets, let everything go that may hinder your enjoying Christ, and let everything be embraced that makes way for your enjoyment of Christ. Oh, let closet prayer be a golden bridge, a ferryboat, a chariot to carry your#super[+] souls over to God, and to bring you into a more intimate communion with God. Let no closet duty satisfy you#super[+] or content you in which you have not conversed with God as a child converses with his father, or as a wife converses with her husband, or as a friend converses with his friend, even face to face. Nothing reveals more unsoundness, falseness, and baseness of heart than this: when men make duty the end of duty, and prayer the end of prayer; when men can begin a duty, and go on in a duty, and close up a duty, and bless and stroke themselves after a duty, and yet never enjoy the least communion with God in the duty.

#emph[Question: But how can a man know whether he has real communion with God in a duty or not?] This is a very noble and necessary question, and accordingly it calls for a clear and satisfying answer; and therefore, I answer thus:

==== 1. A Man May Have Communion with God in Sorrow and Tears

First, A man may have communion with God in sorrow and tears when he does not have communion with God in joy and delight (Ps 51:17). A man may have communion with God in a heart-humbling, a heart-melting, and a heart-abasing way, when he does not have communion with God in a heart-reviving, a heart-cheering, and a heart-comforting way.

It is a very great mistake among many tender-spirited Christians to think that they have no communion with God in their closets unless they meet with God embracing and kissing (Song 2:4–6), cheering and comforting their souls. When they find God raising the springs of joy and comfort in their souls; when they find God speaking peace to them; when they find the special, felt presence of God cheering, refreshing, and enlarging them in their closets, oh, then they are willing to grant that they have had sweet communion with God in their closets. But if God meets with them in their closets and only breaks their hearts for sin and from sin, if He meets with them and only makes His power and His presence known in humbling and casting down their souls, at the sight and sense of their strong corruptions and many imperfections, how unwilling they are to believe that they have had any communion with God!

Well, friends, remember this once and for all: a Christian may have as real communion with God in a heart-humbling way as he can have in a heart-comforting way. A Christian may have as choice communion with God when his eyes are full of tears as he can have when his heart is full of joy (John 20:11–19). Sometimes God meets with a poor Christian in his closet and greatly breaks him and humbles him, and at other times He meets with the same Christian in his closet and mightily cheers him and comforts him. Sometimes God meets with a poor soul in his closet, and there He sweetly quiets him and stills him. And at other times He meets with the same soul in his closet, and then He greatly revives him and enlivens him.

God does not always come upon the soul in one way. He does not always come in at one and the same door (John 3:8). We sometimes look for a friend to come in at the front door, and then he comes in at the back door. And at other times, when we look for him at the back door, then he comes in at the front door. And just so it is with God’s coming into His people’s souls. Sometimes they go into their closets and expect God to come in at the front door of joy and comfort, and then God comes in at the back door of sorrow and grief. And at other times, when they expect God to come in at the back door of humbling, breaking, and melting their hearts, then God comes in at the front door of joy and consolation, cheering and rejoicing their souls.

==== 2. Not All Christians Enjoy the Same Communion in Their Closets

Secondly, I answer that not all Christians enjoy the same communion with God in their closets. Some enjoy much communion with God in their closets, and others enjoy only a little communion with God in their closets.

Moses had a clearer, more glorious, and more constant communion with God in his days than any others had in the times in which he lived (Exod 33:11; Deut 5:4; Num 12:7–8). God spoke to none “face to face” as He did to Moses. And Abraham (Gen 18), in his time, had a closer, friendlier, and more intimate communion with God than holy Lot or any others had in that day. And though all the disciples, Judas excepted, had sweet communion with Christ in the days of His flesh, yet Peter, James, and John had a clearer, choicer, and fuller communion with Him than the rest had (Matt 17:1–4). Among all the disciples, John had the closest bosom communion with Christ. He was the greatest favorite in Christ’s court. He leaned on Christ’s bosom; he could say anything to Christ, and he could know anything of Christ, and he could have anything from Christ (John 13:23; 20:2; 21:20). Now, that not all Christians enjoy communion with God alike in their closets may be made evident in this way:

First, not all Christians #emph[prepare] alike to enjoy closet communion with God; and therefore, not all Christians enjoy communion with God alike in their closets (Eccl 5:1; Ps 10:17). Commonly, he who prepares and fits himself most for closet communion with God is the man who enjoys the most closet communion with God (2 Chr 30:17–20).

Secondly, not all Christians #emph[prize] communion with God in their closets alike. Some prize communion with God in their closets before all and above all other things, as that noble marquis said, “Cursed be he who prefers all the world to one hour’s communion with God.” They look on it as the pearl of great price, for the enjoyment of which they are ready to sell all and part with all. Others prize it at a lower rate, and so enjoy less of it than those who set a higher price and value on it (Job 23:12; Ps 119:127; Matt 13:45–46).

Thirdly, not all Christians #emph[press] after communion with God in their closets alike. Some press after communion with God in their closets as a condemned man presses after a pardon, or as a prisoner in close confinement presses after release, or as a poor beggar presses after alms (Ps 63:8; Isa 26:8–9). Now, you#super[+] know that these press on with the greatest earnestness, the greatest fervency, and the greatest importunity imaginable. But others press after communion with God in their closets more coldly, more carelessly, more slightly, more lazily: “I have taken off my robe—must I put it back on? I have washed my feet—must I soil them again?” (Song 5:3). Now, those who press hardest after communion with God in their closets are usually blessed with the highest degrees of closet communion with God.

Fourthly, not all Christians #emph[make use of] their communion with God in their closets alike; and therefore, not all Christians enjoy communion with God alike in their closets. Some Christians make a wiser, a humbler, a holier, a more faithful, a more fruitful, and a more constant use of their closet communion with God than others do; and therefore, they are blessed with higher degrees of communion with God than others are. Some Christians make more use of their closet communion with God against the world, the flesh, and the devil than others do; and therefore, no wonder if they enjoy more communion with God in their closets than others do.

Fifthly, not all Christians #emph[need] communion with God in their closets alike; and therefore, not all Christians have the same communion with God in their closets. Not all Christians have the same place in the mystical body of Christ (1 Cor 12:14ff): some rule, and others are ruled. Now, every man stands in more or less need of communion with God according to the place that he holds in the body of Christ. Again, not all Christians have the same burdens to bear, nor the same difficulties to contend with, nor the same dangers to escape, nor the same temptations to wrestle with, nor the same passions and corruptions to mortify, nor the same blessings and experiences to make use of; and therefore, not all Christians need communion with God alike in their closets. Now, commonly God imparts Himself more or less in ways of communion as the various necessities and conditions of His people require.

Sixthly and lastly, not all Christians alike #emph[meet with outward interruptions], or inward interruptions; and therefore, not all Christians have communion with God alike in their closets. Some Christians meet with a world of outward and inward interruptions, more than others do. Some Christians’ outward callings, relations, conditions, and stations afford more plentiful matter and occasions to interrupt them in their closet communion with God than other Christians’ callings, relations, conditions, and stations do. Besides, Satan is busier with some Christians than he is with other Christians; and corruptions work more strongly and violently in some Christians than they do in other Christians. And let me add this to all the rest: the very natural dispositions of some Christians are more averse to closet duties than the natural dispositions of other Christians are. And therefore, not all Christians have communion with God alike in their closets, but some have more and some have less, as God in His infinite wisdom sees best.

Now, let no Christian say that he has no communion with God in closet prayer because he does not have such a full, such a choice, such a sweet, such a felt, and such a constant communion with God in closet prayer as such and such saints have had, or as such and such saints now have; for not all saints enjoy communion with God alike in their closets. Some have more, some have less. Some have a higher degree, others a lower. Some are caught up into the third heaven, when others are caught up only into the clouds. What man is so childish and babyish as to argue like this: that he has no wisdom because he does not have the wisdom of Solomon; or that he has no strength because he does not have the strength of Samson; or that he has no life because he does not have the swiftness of Ahimaaz (2 Sam 18:23); or that he has no wealth because he does not have the riches of the rich man? And yet many weak Christians are so childish and babyish as to argue like this: that they have no communion with God in their closets because they do not have such high, such comforting, and such constant communion with God in their closets as such and such saints have had, or as such and such saints now have. They should seriously consider instead that though some saints have a great communion with God, yet other saints have only a small communion with God; and though some Christians have a strong communion with God, yet other Christians have only a weak communion with God; and though some of the people of God have a very close and near communion with God, yet others of the people of God have only a more remote communion with God; and though some of God’s servants have a daily, constant, and uninterrupted communion with God, yet others of His servants have only a more passing and inconstant communion with God.

==== 3. When a Man Exercises Grace in Closet Duties

Thirdly, I answer, When a man exercises grace in closet duties, then certainly he has communion with God in closet duties (2 Tim 1:17; 1 Tim 2:8). When a man in closet duties exercises faith in God, or faith in the promises, or faith in the blood of Christ; or when a man in private duties exercises repentance for sin, or love for Jesus Christ, or sets up God as the object of his fear, or as the object of his joy, then he has communion with God, then he has fellowship with the Father and with the Son (1 John 1:3). An unregenerate man may exercise gifts and talents in a duty, but he cannot exercise grace in a duty. For no man can exercise grace in a duty except he who has grace in his soul. And that is why unsanctified persons, under the highest activity of their skills, talents, and gifts in religious duties, enjoy no communion with God at all. Witness the scribes and Pharisees, Demas, Judas, Simon Magus, etc. (Isa 1:11–13). If ever you#super[+] would have evidence of your communion with God in closet duties, look carefully to the activity of your graces; carefully stir up the grace of God that is in you (2 Tim 1:6).

==== 4. When a Man Gives God the Glory of All His Own Actions

Fourthly, I answer, When a man has communion with God in his closet, then he gives God the glory of all his actions and activities (Ps 115:1). Communion with God always helps a man to set the crown of praise and honor on the head of God. Witness that gracious and grateful doxology of David and his people in 1 Chronicles 29:13, “Now therefore, our God, we give You thanks, and we praise Your glorious name.”

Men who enjoy no communion with God in religious duties are always sacrificing to their own net and burning incense to their own dragnet (Hab 1:16). They are always blessing themselves, and stroking themselves, and applauding themselves. They think the garland of praise, the crown of honor, suits no head but their own (Luke 18:11–12). But now, men who enjoy communion with God in religious duties will uncrown themselves to crown God; they will uncrown their duties to crown the God of their duties; they will uncrown their skills, talents, gifts, and freedom in prayer to set the crown of praise on the head of God alone (Acts 3:11–13, 16; Rev 4:10–11; 5:11–12). You think that you have communion with God in closet duties. Yes, you say that you have communion with God in closet duties; but on whose head do you put the garland of praise (Ps 148:13)? If on God’s head, you have communion with God; if on your own head, you have no communion with God. As all the rivers run into the sea, and all the lines meet in the center, so when all our closet duties end and center in the advancement of God’s glory, then we have communion with God in them.

Constantine used to write the name of Christ over his door. When a man has communion with Christ in a duty, then he will write the name of Christ, the honor of Christ, upon his duty. Some say that the name of Jesus was engraved on the heart of Ignatius. Sure I am that when a man has communion with God in a duty, then you will find the honor and glory of Jesus engraved on that duty.

==== 5. When Closet Duties Leave the Soul in a Better Frame

Fifthly, I answer, When the performance of closet duties leaves the soul in a better frame, then a man has communion with God in them. When a man comes away from closet duties in a more holy frame, or in a more humble frame, or in a more spiritual frame, or in a more watchful frame, or in a more heavenly frame, or in a more broken frame, or in a more revived and enlivened frame, then certainly he has had communion with God in those duties. When a man comes out of his closet and finds the frame of his heart more strongly set against sin than ever, and more highly resolved to walk with God than ever, and more eminently crucified to the world than ever, and more divinely fixed against temptations than ever, then beyond all doubt he has had communion with God in his closet.

==== 6. When Closet Duties Fit a Man for the Duties Next at Hand

Sixthly, I answer, When closet duties fit a man for the other duties that lie next at hand, then doubtless he has had communion with God in them. When private duties fit a man for public duties, or when private duties fit a man for the duties of his place, calling, and condition, in which God has set him, then certainly he has had fellowship with God in them (Eccl 9:10). When a man in closet duties finds more spiritual strength and power to perform the duties that are next required of him, then assuredly he has met with God. When private prayer fits me better for family prayer or public prayer, then I may safely conclude that God has drawn near to my soul in private prayer. Or when one closet duty fits me for another closet duty, as when praying fits me for reading, or reading for praying; or when the more external duties in my closet (reading or praying) fit me for the more spiritual and internal duties (self-examination, holy meditation, soul-humbling), then I may rest satisfied that there has been some choice fellowship between God and my soul. When the more I pray in my closet the fitter I am to pray in my closet; and the more I read in my closet the fitter I am to read in my closet; and the more I meditate in my closet the fitter I am to meditate in my closet; and the more I search and examine my heart in my closet the fitter I am to search and examine my heart in my closet; and the more I humble and abase my soul in my closet the fitter I am to humble and abase my soul in my closet, then I may be confident that I have had communion with God in my closet.

==== 7. Private Communion with God Is Soul-Humbling and Soul-Abasing

Seventhly, I answer that all private communion with God is very soul-humbling and soul-abasing. Abraham was a man who had much private communion with God, and a man who was very vile and low in his own eyes. “Then Abraham answered, ‘Now that I have ventured to speak to the Lord—though I am but dust and ashes’” (Gen 18:27). “In respect of my origin,” says Abraham, “I am but base dust and ashes. And in respect of my deserts, I deserve to be burned to ashes.” There are none so humble as those who have the nearest communion with God (Gen 28:10–18). Jacob was a man who had much private communion with God, and a man who was very little in his own eyes. “I am unworthy of the least of all the mercies and all the faithfulness You have shown Your servant” (Gen 32:10), or, as the Hebrew has it, “I am less than all Your mercies.” When Jacob had to deal with Laban, he pleads his merit; but when he has to do with God, he abases himself below the least of His mercies (Gen 31:38–41). Moses was a man who had much private communion with God, as I have shown before, and a man who was the meekest and humblest person in all the world. “Now Moses was a very humble man, more so than any man on the face of the earth” (Num 12:3).

Josephus, writing of Moses, says, if he may be believed, “that he was so free from passions that he knew no such thing in his own soul. He knew passions only by their names, and saw them in others, but did not feel them in himself.” And so, when the glory of God appeared to him, he falls on his face (Num 16:22) as a sign of humility and self-abasement. David was a man who had much private communion with God, as all agree; and how greatly he abases and belittles himself! “The king of Israel has come out to look for a flea” (1 Sam 26:20); and what is weaker and more contemptible than a flea? “Against whom has the king of Israel come out? Whom are you pursuing? A dead dog? A flea?” (1 Sam 24:14). It is as if David had said, “It is not worth the time and the labor. It is below the dignity and honor of the king of Israel to take such pains, and to pursue so violently, such a poor nothing as I am, who have no more strength or power to bite or hurt than a dead dog or a poor flea has.” “But I am a worm and not a man” (Ps 22:6). Now, what is weaker, what less regarded, what more despicable, what more trampled underfoot than a poor worm? The Hebrew word #emph[tolagnath], here rendered “worm,” signifies a very little worm, such as breeds in scarlet, so little that a man can scarcely see it or perceive it. Thus you see that holy David lowers himself below a worm, indeed, below the least of worms. No man sets so low a value on himself as he who has the most private communion with God. The twenty-four elders cast down their crowns at the feet of Jesus Christ (Rev 4:10–11). Their crowns signify all their inward and outward dignities, excellencies, and glories. And the casting down of their crowns signifies their great humility and self-abasement. When Christians, in their closets and out of their closets, can cast down their crowns, their duties, their services, their graces, their enlargements, their enjoyments at the feet of Jesus Christ, and sit down abasing and belittling themselves, then certainly they have had a very near and sweet communion with God.

Chrysostom has a remarkable saying about humility: “Suppose,” he says, “that one man were defiled with every kind of sin and enormity, yet humble, and another man enriched with gifts, graces, and duties, yet proud. The humble sinner would be in a safer condition than this proud saint.”

When a man can come away from closet duties and say, as Ignatius once said of himself, #emph[Non sum dignus dici minimus], I am not worthy to be called the least, then certainly he has had fellowship with God in them. All the communion that a man has with God in his closet is very soul-humbling and soul-abasing. In all a man’s communion with God, some beams, some rays of the glory and majesty of God will shine on his soul. Now all divine manifestations are very humbling and abasing, as you may clearly see in those two great instances of Job and Isaiah. “My ears had heard of You, but now my eyes have seen You. Therefore I abhor myself, and I repent in dust and ashes” (Job 42:5–6). “In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord seated on a throne, high and exalted; and the train of His robe filled the temple.…Then I said: ‘Woe is me, for I am ruined, because I am a man of unclean lips dwelling among a people of unclean lips; for my eyes have seen the King, the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts’” (Isa 6:1, 5). What sweet communion Elijah had with God in the low cave!

There was a gentlewoman of no ordinary rank or upbringing who, being much troubled in mind and grievously deserted by God, could not be persuaded by her husband or any other Christian friends either to hear or to read anything that might work for her spiritual good. At last her husband, by much importunity, prevailed so far with her that she was willing for him to read one chapter of the Bible to her. So he read Isaiah 57, and came to the fifteenth verse: “For thus says the One who is high and lifted up, who inhabits eternity, whose name is Holy: ‘I dwell in a high and holy place, and with the contrite and humble in spirit, to restore the spirit of the lowly and revive the heart of the contrite.’” “Oh,” she said, “is it so, that God dwells with a contrite and humble spirit? Then I am sure He dwells with me, for my heart is broken into a thousand pieces. Oh, happy text and happy time, that I should ever hear such comfort!” And with that she recovered. The more communion any man has with God, the more humble and broken his heart will be.

Holy Bradford was a man who had much private communion with God, and he would often sign his letters, “John the hypocrite, and a very painted sepulcher.” Agur was one of the wisest and holiest men on the earth in his days, and he condemned himself as “more brutish than any man,” and as one who lacks “the understanding of a man” (Prov 30:2). How much sweeter is the smell of the lowly violet that hides its head than all the gaudy tulips in your garden! The lowly Christian is the most attractive and the loveliest Christian. When a man can come out of his closet and cry out with Augustine, “I hate what I am, and love and desire what I am not. O wretched man that I am, in whom the cross of Christ has not yet eaten out the poisonous and bitter taste of the first tree”; or, as another says, “Lord, I see, and yet am blind. I will, and yet rebel. I hate, and yet I love. I follow, and yet I fall. I press forward, yet I faint. I wrestle, yet I limp”; then he may be confident that he has had communion with God in his closet. He who comes away from closet duties in a self-abasing way, laying himself low at the foot of God, has certainly had communion with God. But when men come out of their closets with their hearts swelled and lifted up, as the hearts of the Pharisees were (Luke 18:11–12), it is evident that they have had no communion with God. God has not been near to the souls of those who say, “Keep to yourself; do not come near me, for I am holier than you!” (Isa 65:5).

==== 8. When It Wounds and Weakens His Beloved Corruption

Eighthly, and lastly, when a man finds such a secret power and force running through his closet duties that it wounds and weakens his beloved corruption, breaks the strength and power of his special sin, sets his heart more fully, resolutely, and constantly against his darling lust, and stirs up a greater rage, a more bitter hatred, and a fiercer indignation against the toad in the bosom, then certainly he has had communion with God in his closet duties.

Consult this scripture: “In that day men will cast away to the moles and bats their idols of silver and gold—the idols they made to worship” (Isa 2:20). In the day when God would take these poor hearts into communion with Himself, their hearts would be filled with such rage and indignation against their most delightful and desirable idols that they would take not only those made of wood and stone, but even their most precious and costly idols, those made of silver and gold, and cast them to the moles and to the bats, to show their horrible hatred and indignation against them. Idolatry was the darling sin of the Jews. Their hearts were so exceedingly taken with their idols, and delighted in them, that they did not care what they spent on them. “They lavish gold from the bag and weigh out silver on scales; they hire a goldsmith to fashion it into a god, so they can bow down and worship” (Isa 46:6). The word used here in the Hebrew for #emph[lavish] properly signifies to waste or spend riotously. They valued their treasure so lightly that they did not care what they spent on their idols. God gave them gold and silver as pledges of His favor and bounty, and they lavish it on their idols, as if God had hired them to be wicked. Oh, but when God would come and take these poor wretches into close and near communion with Himself, then you will find their wrath and rage rising against their idols, as you may see in Isaiah 30:19–21. Their communion with God is more than hinted at there; but mark verse 22: “You#super[+] will defile your silver-plated idols and your gold-plated images. You will throw them away like a menstrual cloth, saying to them, ‘Be gone!’” None defile, deface, detest, and disgrace their idols like those who are taken into communion with God. Fellowship with God will make a man throw away, like a menstrual cloth, the very idols in which he has most delighted, and with which he has been most pleased and enamored.

Idols were Ephraim’s bosom sin. “Ephraim is joined,” or glued, as the Hebrew has it, “to idols; leave him alone!” (Hos 4:17). Oh, but when you find Ephraim taken into close communion with God, as you do in Hosea 14:4–7, then you will find another spirit on him. “Ephraim shall say, ‘What have I to do anymore with idols?’” (Hos 14:8). I have had too much to do with them already. I will never have anything to do with them again. Oh, how my soul detests and abhors them and rises up against them! Oh, how I now loathe and abominate them more than ever I loved them or delighted in them before! After the return of the Jews from Babylon, they so hated and abhorred idols that in the time of the Romans they chose to die rather than allow the eagle, the imperial standard, to be set up in their temple.

Though closet duties are weak in themselves, yet when a man has communion with God in them, they prove exceedingly powerful for casting down strongholds, and vain imaginations, and every high thing and thought “set up against the knowledge of God” (2 Cor 10:4–5). When a man comes out of his closet with a heart more fully and steadfastly set against every known sin, but especially against his bosom sin, his darling sin, his Delilah that he played and toyed with most, and that he has hugged in his bosom with pleasure and delight, then certainly he has had private communion with God.

After Moses had enjoyed forty days’ private communion with God on the mountain, how his heart rose and his anger burned against the molten calf that his people had made! “As Moses approached the camp and saw the calf and the dancing, he burned with anger and threw the tablets out of his hands, shattering them at the base of the mountain. Then he took the calf they had made, burned it in the fire, ground it to powder, and scattered the powder over the face of the water. Then he forced the Israelites to drink it” (Exod 32:19–20). Moses never had more intimate fellowship with God than now, and he never showed so much holy zeal, anger, and indignation against sin as now. When a man comes down from the mountain of closet duties with a greater hatred, anger, wrath, and indignation against bosom sins, darling sins, the sins of his own temperament, that were once as dear to him as right hands or right eyes, or as Delilah was to Samson, or Herodias to Herod, or Isaac to Abraham, or Joseph to Jacob, then certainly he has had communion with God in those duties. When a man finds his beloved sins, his Delilahs, which, like the prince of devils, command all other sins, fall before his closet duties, as Dagon fell before the ark, or as Goliath fell before David, then assuredly he has had fellowship with God in them. Pliny writes of some families that had secret marks on their bodies, found only in those of that line. Certainly there are no families, no persons, but have some sin or sins, some secret marks on their souls, that may in a special way be called theirs. Now when in private duties they find the bent of their hearts, and the purposes, resolutions, and inclinations of their souls, more raised, inflamed, and set against these, they may safely and comfortably conclude that they have had communion with God in them. O sirs! There is no bosom sin so sweet or profitable that it is worth burning in hell for, or worth being shut out of heaven for. And therefore, in all your#super[+] private duties and services, labor after such communion with God in them as may break the neck and heart of your most bosom sins. When Darius fled before Alexander, he threw his heavy crown from his head, so that he might run out of danger the faster. If ever you#super[+] would be safe from eternal danger, throw away your golden and your silver idols; throw away your bosom sins, your darling lusts.

And so I have finished the answers to that noble and necessary question that was last proposed.

=== i. See That the Glory of God Is Your Ultimate End

My ninth advice and counsel is this: in all your#super[+] closet duties, see that your ends are right; see that the glory of God is your ultimate end, the mark, the center of the target, that you have in your eye. There is great truth in that old saying, #emph[Quod non actibus, sed finibus pensantur officia], “Duties are valued not by their acts but by their ends.”

As the shining sun puts out the light of the fire, so the glory of God must consume all other ends. There may be #emph[malum opus in bona materia] (a bad work in a good matter), as in Jehu’s zeal. Two things make a good Christian: good actions and good aims. And though a good aim does not make a bad action good, as in Uzzah, yet a bad aim makes a good action bad, as in Jehu, whose justice was approved but his scheming punished. God writes “nothing” over all those services in which men’s ends are not right. “They did not obey Your voice or walk in Your law. They have done nothing of all that You commanded them to do” (Jer 32:23). “All this disaster has come upon us, yet we have not made our prayer before the #smallcaps[Lord] our God” (Dan 9:13). The Jews were very much in religious duties and services; witness Isa 1:11–15; Isa 58:1–3; Zech 7:5–6. I might produce a hundred more witnesses to confirm it, were it necessary. But because they did not aim at the glory of God in what they did, the Lord writes “nothing” over all their duties and services. It was Ephraim’s folly that he brought forth fruit for himself (Hos 10:1). And it was the Pharisees’ hypocrisy that in all their duties and services they looked to the praise of men. “Truly I tell you#super[+],” says Christ, “they already have their full reward” (Matt 6:1–5). A poor, a pitiful reward indeed! Men who cannot look above the praises of men will be sure to fall short of divine acceptance and of a glorious recompense. Woe to the man who, like Augustus, is ambitious to go off the stage of duty to applause. Peter was not himself when he denied his Lord and cursed himself to gain credit among a cursed crew. If ever you#super[+] would ask and have, speak and succeed, seek and find, see that the glory of the Lord is engraved on all your closet duties.

He will be sure to fare best whose heart is set most on glorifying God in all his secret retirements. When God crowns us, He only crowns His own gifts in us; and when we give God the glory of all we do, we only give Him the glory that is due to His name. For it is He, and He alone, who works all our works in us and for us. All closet duties are good or bad according to the mark at which the soul aims. He who makes God the object of closet prayer, but not the end of closet prayer, only loses his prayer and takes pains to undo himself. God will be Alexander or Nemo; He will be all in all, or He will be nothing at all.

Prayers that are not directed to the glory of God never reach the ear of God, nor delight the heart of God, nor will ever be lodged in the bosom of God. The end must be as noble as the means, or else a man may be undone after all his doings. A man’s most glorious actions will at last be found to be only glorious sins if he has made himself, and not the glory of God, the end of those actions.

=== j. Offer All Your Closet Prayers in Christ’s Name, and in His Alone

My tenth advice and counsel is this: be sure that you#super[+] offer all your closet prayers in Christ’s name, and in His alone. “And I will do whatever you#super[+] ask in My name, so that the Father may be glorified in the Son. If you#super[+] ask Me for anything in My name, I will do it” (John 14:13–14). “So that whatever you#super[+] ask the Father in My name, He will give you” (John 15:16). “Truly, truly, I tell you#super[+], whatever you ask the Father in My name, He will give you. Until now you#super[+] have not asked for anything in My name. Ask and you#super[+] will receive, so that your joy may be complete.…In that day you will ask in My name. I am not saying that I will ask the Father on your#super[+] behalf” (John 16:23–24, 26). O sirs! This is your#super[+] privilege as well as your comfort, that you never deal with God except through a mediator. When you#super[+] appear before God, Jesus Christ appears with you, and He appears for you. When you#super[+] do #emph[invocare], call on, pray, then He does #emph[advocare], call out to, advocate. When you#super[+] put up your petitions, then He makes intercession for you. Christ gives you#super[+] a commission to put His name on all your requests; and whatever prayer comes up with this name on it, He will procure an answer for it.

In the state of innocence, man might worship God without a mediator; but since sin has made so wide a breach between God and man, God will accept no worship from man but what is offered up by the hand of a mediator. Now this mediator is Christ alone. “For there is one God, and there is one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (1 Tim 2:5). One mediator, not of redemption only, as the papists grant, but of intercession also, which they deny.

The papists make saints and angels co-mediators with Christ; but in this, as in other things, they fight against the clear light of Scripture. The apostle plainly tells us that the office of intercession belongs to Christ as part of His mediation (Heb 7:25). And it is certain that we need no other officer to present our requests in heaven but the man Christ Jesus, who, being so near to the Father, and so dear to the Father, and so much in favor with the Father, can doubtless win anything from the Father that makes for His glory and our good. This was typified in the law. The high priest alone entered the sanctuary and carried the names of the children of Israel before the Lord, while the people all stood outside. This pointed to Christ’s mediation (Exod 28:29). In Leviticus 16:13–14, you read of two things: first, the cloud of incense that covered the mercy-seat; secondly, the blood of the bull that was sprinkled before the mercy-seat. Now that blood typified Christ’s satisfaction, and the cloud of incense His intercession.

Some of the learned think that Christ intercedes only by virtue of His merits; others, that He does it only with His mouth. I conjecture it may be done both ways, all the more because Christ has a tongue, as well as a whole body, though glorified, in heaven. And is it likely that the mouth which pleaded so much for us on earth should be altogether silent for us in heaven?

There is no coming to the Father except through the Son (John 14:6). Christ is the true Jacob’s ladder by which we must ascend to heaven. Joseph, you know, commanded his brothers that, if ever they hoped for any good from him or to see his face with joy, they must be sure to bring their brother Benjamin with them. O sirs! If ever you#super[+] would prevail with God, if ever you would have sweet, choice, and comforting returns from heaven to all your closet prayers, be sure that you bring your elder brother, the Lord Jesus Christ, in the arms of your faith. Be sure that you#super[+] treat and trade with God only in the name of the Lord Jesus.

It is a notable saying that Luther has on the 130th Psalm: “Often and willingly,” he says, “do I drive this home: that you#super[+] should shut your eyes and your ears, and say you know no God outside Christ, none but Him who was in the lap of Mary and nursed at her breasts.” #emph[Dulce nomen Christi] (Sweet is the name of Christ). He means none outside Him. When you#super[+] go to closet prayer, see that you do not pray in your own names, but in the name of Christ; and that you do not plead in your own names, but in the name of Christ; and that you do not believe and hope in your own names, but in the name of Christ; and that you do not look to succeed in your own names, but in the name of Christ. “And whatever you#super[+] do, in word or deed, do it all in the name of the Lord Jesus” (Col 3:17). Whatever we do, we are to do it by the authority of Christ, and through the help of Christ, and in the name of Christ, and for the sake and glory of Christ.

Christ’s name is so precious and powerful with the Father that it will carry any suit and obtain any request at His hands. Jesus, in the Chinese tongue, signifies the rising sun. When a man writes the name of Jesus on his closet prayers, he will be sure to succeed. Though God will not give a man a drop, a sip, a crumb, a crust, for his own sake, yet for Jesus’ sake He will give the best, the choicest, and the greatest blessings that heaven affords. That name is always mighty and powerful, prevailing and precious before the Lord. The prayers that were offered up with the incense on the altar were pleasing (Rev 8:3) and came up with acceptance (v. 4). Joseph’s brothers were kindly treated for Benjamin’s sake.

O sirs! All our duties and services are accepted by the Father, not for their own sakes, nor for our sakes, but for Christ’s sake. No prayers are heard, acknowledged, accepted, regarded, or rewarded but those that Christ puts His hand to. If Christ does not mingle His blood with our sacrifices, our services, they will be lost, and will never ascend as incense before the Lord. No coin is current that does not have Caesar’s stamp on it; and no prayers pass current in heaven that do not have the stamp of Christ on them. Nothing is more pleasing to our heavenly Father than for us to use the mediation of His Son. Those will be sure to find most favor and to fare best in the court of heaven who always present themselves before the Father with Christ in their arms.

=== k. Closely Watch What Becomes of Your Private Prayers

My eleventh and last advice and counsel is this: when you#super[+] come out of your closets, closely watch what becomes of your private prayers. Look at what door, in what way, and by what hand the Lord will be pleased to give you#super[+] an answer to the secret desires of your souls in a corner. It has been the custom of the people of God to look after their prayers, to see what success they have had, to observe what reception they have found in heaven.

“In the morning, O #smallcaps[Lord], You hear my voice; in the morning I will direct my prayer to You and will look up” (Ps 5:3). In these words you may observe two things: first, David’s posture in prayer; secondly, his practice after prayer. First, his posture in prayer: I will “direct my prayer to You.” Secondly, his practice after prayer: I “will look up.” The prophet in these words makes use of two military words. First, he would not only pray, but marshal his prayers; he would put them in battle array. That is what the Hebrew word #emph[gnarach], translated “direct,” imports. Secondly, when he had done this, he would be like a spy on his watchtower, to see whether he prevailed, whether he won the day or not. That is what the Hebrew word #emph[tsaphah], translated “look up,” imports. When David had set his prayers, his petitions, in rank and file, in good array, he was resolved to look out, to look about him, to see at what door God would send in an answer to prayer. He is either a fool or a madman, either very weak or very wicked, who prays and prays but never looks after his prayers, who shoots many an arrow toward heaven but never watches where his arrows land.

“I will listen to what God the #smallcaps[Lord] will say; for He will surely speak peace to His people and His saints” (Ps 85:8). If David would have God listen to his prayers, he must then listen to what God will speak; and on this point it seems he was fully resolved. You have the prophet’s prayer in the first seven verses of this psalm, and his gracious resolution in the eighth verse: “I will listen to what God the #smallcaps[Lord] will say.” As if he had said, “Certainly it will not be long before the Lord gives me a gracious answer, a timely and a fitting return to my present prayers.”

“Out of the depths I cry to You, O #smallcaps[Lord]! O Lord, hear my voice; let Your ears be attentive to my plea for mercy.…I wait for the #smallcaps[Lord]; my soul does wait, and in His word I put my hope. My soul waits for the Lord more than watchmen wait for the morning—more than watchmen wait for the morning” (Ps 130:1–2, 5–6). Those who keep watch outside in dangerous times and wearisome weather look often for the first light of day. How the weary sentry who is wet with the rain of heaven or with the dew of the night waits and watches, looks and longs, for the morning light! Now this was the frame and disposition of David’s spirit when he came away from praying: he falls to waiting for a gracious answer. Shall the farmer wait for the precious fruits of the earth, and shall the merchant wait for the return of his ships, and shall the wife wait for the return of her husband who has gone on a long journey, and shall not a Christian wait for the return of his prayers (Jas 5:7–8)? As Noah patiently waited for the return of the dove to the ark with an olive branch in its mouth, so must you#super[+] patiently wait for the return of your prayers. When children shoot their arrows, they never watch where they fall; but when prudent archers shoot their arrows up into the air, they stand and watch where they fall. You#super[+] must deal with your prayers as prudent archers do with their arrows.

“I will stand at my guard post and station myself on the ramparts. I will watch to see what He will say to me” (Hab 2:1). The prophet, having been very earnest in his complaints to God and very fervent in his supplications in the chapter before, now gets up on his watchtower to see what becomes of his prayers. He stands as a sentry and watches as vigilantly and as carefully as a spy, a scout, earnestly longing to hear and see the outcome, the issue, and the success of his prayers.

The Christian who in prayer has one eye on a divine precept and another on a gracious promise will be sure to look after his prayers. He who prays and waits, and waits and prays, will be sure to succeed. He will never fail of rich returns (Ps 40:1–4). He who can go without as well as wait, and who can be content that God is glorified though he himself is not gratified; he who dares not run ahead of God’s promises, but patiently waits for their fulfillment, may be confident that he will have timely and fitting answers to all the prayers he has sent off to heaven. Though God seldom comes at our time, yet He never fails to come at His own time. “He who is coming will come and will not delay” (Heb 10:37). The mercies of God are not called “the swift,” but “the sure” mercies of David (Isa 55:3). He who makes it as much a matter of conscience to look after his prayers as to pray will shortly clap his hands for joy and cry out with that blessed martyr, “He has come, Austin, He has come, He has come.”

Certainly there is little worth in that man’s heart or in that man’s prayers who keeps up a trade of prayer but never looks to see what becomes of his prayers. When you#super[+] are in your closets, marshal your prayers. See that every prayer keeps its place and ground; and when you#super[+] come out of your closets, then look up for an answer. Only take care that you#super[+] are not too hasty and impatient with God. Though the mercy in the promise is yours#super[+], yet the time of giving it out is the Lord’s. And therefore you#super[+] must wait as well as pray.

And so much by way of counsel and advice for the better carrying on of closet prayer.
